---
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Will blocking AI crawlers hurt my traffic?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Your Google rankings are unaffected. What changes is presence in AI answers, which is a business decision per vendor, not an SEO penalty." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Which AI crawlers ignore robots.txt?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Bytespider is reported to ignore it at times. For that one, edge-level blocking by autonomous system is the enforcement that holds." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Can I block AI crawlers in Cloudflare?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Yes: the managed AI scraper blocking control, or a custom WAF rule on the user-agent token or ASN, blocks at the edge before robots.txt is even read." } }
  ],
};
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

---
<BaseLayout
  schema={[faqSchema]}
  title="How to Block AI Crawlers (robots.txt, WAF, and DNS)"
  description="How to block AI crawlers: the exact robots.txt rules for GPTBot, ClaudeBot, Google-Extended and friends, plus WAF-level blocking for bots that ignore robots.txt."
  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["AI Web Infrastructure", "/ai-web-infrastructure/"], ["Block AI Crawlers"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ai-web-infrastructure/" class="hover:text-accent">AI Web Infrastructure</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Block AI Crawlers</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">How to Block AI Crawlers</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      Keeping AI systems off your content is a three-layer decision: robots.txt for the polite ones,
      WAF rules for the ones that misbehave, and legal/technical walls for everything else. Here is
      each layer, exactly.
    </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Block rules per crawler</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Crawler</th><th>Rule</th><th>Enforcement</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">GPTBot</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Disallow: /</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Honored</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">ClaudeBot</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Disallow: /</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Honored</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Google-Extended</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Disallow: /</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Controls training only</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Bytespider</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Disallow: /</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Reported unreliable; use edge rules</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#layer-1-robotstxt-works-for-compliant-bo">Layer 1: robots.txt (works for compliant bots)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#layer-2-waf-and-network-rules-for-bots-t">Layer 2: WAF and network rules (for bots that ignore robots.txt)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#layer-3-authentication-and-paywalls">Layer 3: authentication and paywalls</a></li>
        <li><a href="#before-you-block-everything">Before you block everything</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="layer-1-robotstxt-works-for-compliant-bo">Layer 1: robots.txt (works for compliant bots)</h2>
      <p>Drop this block into your robots.txt to refuse the major AI training crawlers:</p>
      <pre class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">User-agent: GPTBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: OAI-SearchBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: ClaudeBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: Google-Extended
Disallow: /

User-agent: PerplexityBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: CCBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: Bytespider
Disallow: /

User-agent: Applebot-Extended
Disallow: /

User-agent: meta-externalagent
Disallow: /</pre>
      <p>
        Note what is deliberately absent: Googlebot. Blocking it removes you from Google Search and
        AI Overviews alike, which almost no site wants. Verify your rules with the
        <a href="/ai-crawler-checker/">AI Crawler Checker</a> after saving.
      </p>

      <h2 id="layer-2-waf-and-network-rules-for-bots-t">Layer 2: WAF and network rules (for bots that ignore robots.txt)</h2>
      <p>
        robots.txt is a convention, not a fence. Bots with poor compliance reputations (Bytespider
        historically among them) may fetch anyway. Cloudflare customers can enable managed AI-bot
        rules or write a firewall rule on the user-agent string; any WAF can match
        <code>User-Agent contains "Bytespider"</code> and issue a challenge or block. This layer
        actually enforces.
      </p>

      <h2 id="layer-3-authentication-and-paywalls">Layer 3: authentication and paywalls</h2>
      <p>
        Content behind a login is not crawled, period. If your model is subscription content, the
        reliable block is authentication plus rate limiting, with robots rules as the courtesy layer
        on top.
      </p>

      <h2 id="before-you-block-everything">Before you block everything</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Blocking training crawlers does not remove you from search-style answers unless you also block their search bots (OAI-SearchBot, PerplexityBot).</li>
        <li>Aggressive wildcard Disallow rules often take out legitimate crawlers with them; test changes with the <a href="/robots-txt-checker/">robots.txt checker</a>.</li>
        <li>Vendors also run opt-out portals (OpenAI, Google) that operate independently of robots.txt.</li>
      </ul>

      
<h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/how-to-allow-ai-crawlers/">How to allow AI crawlers</a> (the other direction)</li>
        <li><a href="/ai-crawlers-list/">The complete list of AI crawlers</a></li>
        <li>Per-crawler guides: <a href="/gptbot/">GPTBot</a>, <a href="/claudebot/">ClaudeBot</a>, <a href="/google-extended/">Google-Extended</a>, <a href="/perplexitybot/">PerplexityBot</a>, <a href="/oai-searchbot/">OAI-SearchBot</a>, <a href="/ccbot/">CCBot</a>, <a href="/bytespider/">Bytespider</a></li>
        <li><a href="/ai-crawler-verification/">Verify a crawler with reverse DNS</a></li>
        <li><a href="/block-ai-crawlers-cloudflare/">Block AI crawlers with Cloudflare</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Will blocking AI crawlers hurt my traffic?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Your Google rankings are unaffected. What changes is presence in AI answers, which is a business decision per vendor, not an SEO penalty.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Which AI crawlers ignore robots.txt?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Bytespider is reported to ignore it at times. For that one, edge-level blocking by autonomous system is the enforcement that holds.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I block AI crawlers in Cloudflare?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: the managed AI scraper blocking control, or a custom WAF rule on the user-agent token or ASN, blocks at the edge before robots.txt is even read.</p></details></div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
